  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe latest surface analysis shows a cold front extending across\nsouthern PA through central/southern Ohio. This front will\ncontinue to track southeastward this evening before exiting the\narea. A band of showers in the vicinity of the front will \ncontinue until FROPA later this evening. The heavy rainfall and\nsevere weather potential have ended, with diminishing\ninstability and warming cloud tops. \n\nPartial clearing is expected overnight as high pressure begins\nto build in. This clearing, and diminishing wind, should result\nin radiational cooling and areas of fog overnight, especially\nwith the saturated ground in place. The fog should dissipate\nwith mixing and heating Tuesday morning. \n\nTuesday will be dry areawide with quasi-zonal mid-level flow and \nweak surface high pressure providing a comfortable mid-August day \nwith highs in the upper 70s and low 60s dew points.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nOur one day of relief from showers and storms quickly fades \ninto the past as yet another cold front moves through on \nWednesday. Return flow will pull better Gulf moisture back into \nthe local area again, though not to the extent we saw over the \npast weekend, with ensemble mean PWAT values climbing back up to\n~1.5\" and dew points to the upper 60s. Right entrance region \njet dynamics and weak PVA will aid in synoptic ascent, and a 40+\nknot mid-level jet increase deep layer shear. That said, \nsurface CAPE appears to be a primary limiting factor with NBM \nmean around 300-600 J/kg and only getting to 1000 J/kg with the \n90th percentile values contingent low probability clearing in \nthe morning and push of higher dew points. CIPS analogs is not \nexcited about any severe or flooding with the front, so we \nshouldn't be looking at any significant threat either way on \nWednesday.\n\nThe question beyond that point is the progression, or lackthereof, \nof the front. Mid-level flow turns more zonal and subsequently more \nparallel to the front suggesting it slows/stalls absent any \nmechanism to give it a kick to keep moving. NBM dew point spread as \na proxy for boundary placement basically suggests it could stall \nanywhere from I-80 to I-70, though ensemble clusters lean toward \nslow progression south Thursday and Friday focusing the best rain \nchances along and south Pittsburgh by the second half of the day on \nThursday. Obviously will have to watch for repeated rounds of \nrain over the same areas, but generally weak forcing might lend \nmore toward scattered, diurnally driven showers and storms with \nthe highest coverage on Thursday afternoon as a shortwave tracks\nacross the boundary. NBM 90th percentile 24 hour totals sit \nabout as high as 1.75\", so the rainfall totals ceiling should \nbe a bit lower than we've seen in the past week. Machine \nlearning pegs a lower end flood risk.\n\nSaid shortwave likely gives the stalled boundary the punch it \nneeds to exit our area by the weekend, though a troughy pattern\nlikely keeps us active with longer range AI & ML guidance \nsuggesting our next severe weather risk popping up on Saturday.",
